## Directory structure

```
cmd/main.go              # Entry point — wires everything
internal/
  features/
    {{moduleName}}/      # One directory per feature — all layers together
      handler.go         # HTTP handlers
      service.go         # Business logic
      repository.go      # Data access
      model.go           # Domain model
      dto.go             # Request/Response DTOs
  config/                # App configuration
  errors/                # Error types and HTTP error helpers
  middleware/            # net/http middleware (logging, recovery, CORS)
  shared/                # Shared infrastructure (DB, cache, health)
  docs/                  # Generated Swagger docs
```

## Hard rules

1. **No DI container.** Each feature directory contains its own wiring. The
   feature package exports a `RegisterRoutes` function.
2. **One directory per feature.** Everything a feature needs — handler,
   service, repository, model, DTOs — lives in a single directory.
3. **`cmd/` is for entry points**, `internal/features/` is for feature code,
   `internal/shared/` is for cross-cutting infrastructure.
4. **Adding a feature means creating one directory** with all the files for
   that feature.
5. **Register new features** in `cmd/main.go` by importing and calling
   `feature.RegisterRoutes`.
6. **Handlers never touch the repository directly.** Always go through the
   service layer.
7. **`config/config.go` is the only file that reads `os.Getenv` directly.**
8. **Handlers are plain `http.HandlerFunc`** — path params from `r.PathValue("id")`.
{{#if ormGorm}}
9. **GORM models** use pointer receivers for `Save`/`Delete`.
{{/if}}
{{#if useAuth}}
10. **Protected routes** wrap `/api/` prefix. User claims stored in
    `r.Context()` under `middleware.UserContextKey`.
{{/if}}
{{#if useSwagger}}
11. **Swagger annotations** go on handler methods.
{{/if}}

## When you are unsure

Prefer asking over guessing. Generating a plausible-looking file in the wrong
layer costs more to unpick than a clarifying question costs to answer.
